.SH NAME
md__r_e_a_d_m_e \- Open-CSMP++ README 
.PP


.PP
Some background\&. CSMP (Complex Systems Modeling Platform) is a C++17 ANSI / ISO standard-compliant application programmer interface (API)\&. It is a library of numerical methods for the combined simulation of thermal–hydrological–mechanical–chemical (THMC) processes, and has been designed for complex models of the subsurface, spanning a range of length scales\&.

.PP
This CSMP repository contains implementations of the finite-element- (FEM) and finite volume (FVM) methods and combinations thereof\&. In addition to solution methods for elliptic-parabolic and hyberbolic partial differential equations, it provides implementations of:

.PP
CSMP's' unique data model supporting computations on regions, boundaries and internal discontinuities\&. Material properties and dependent variables stored using a tree structures representing the mesh\&. Computational domains can evolve in shape and properties over time, and multi-domain simulations, where different physics are modelled in different regions, can be implemented\&.

.PP
Constitutive relationships for THMC processes Equations of state Property modeling and simulation analysis functionality Local (asynchronous) time-stepping methods and support for dynamic mesh adaptivity, facilitating goal-based simulation\&.

.PP
CSMP forms the basis of a wide variety of simulation programs, including discrete fracture and matrix modelling, upscaling and reservoir simulation\&. Publications on these applications are furnished upon request\&.
.SH "Quick Start"
.PP
.IP "1." 4
\fBClone the repository:\fP 
.PP
.nf
git clone https://gitlab\&.com/csmp/open\-csmp\-2024\&.git
cd open\-csmp\-2024

.fi
.PP

.IP "2." 4
\fBCreate and enter build directory:\fP 
.PP
.nf
mkdir build && cd build

.fi
.PP

.IP "3." 4
\fBConfigure with CMake:\fP 
.PP
.nf
cmake \&.\&. \-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release

.fi
.PP

.IP "4." 4
\fBBuild the project:\fP 
.PP
.nf
make \-j$(nproc)  # For Linux
make \-j$(sysctl \-n hw\&.ncpu)  # For macOS

.fi
.PP

.IP "5." 4
\fBRun tests (optional):\fP 
.PP
.nf
ctest

.fi
.PP

.IP "6." 4
\fBStart with examples:\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Check the \fRexamples/\fP directory for sample code
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Copy \fRexamples/example_inputs/\fP to a new location for your own experiments
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Use \fRexamples/Experimental_Example\&.cpp\fP as a template for new development
.PP

.PP

.PP
For more detailed instructions, see the \fBBuilding CSMP\fP section below\&.
.SH "Installation"
.PP
CSMP can be installed to a custom location using CMake's installation feature\&. This is useful for system-wide or user-specific installations\&.
.SS "User-specific Installation (Recommended for Development)"
.IP "1." 4
\fBCreate an installation directory:\fP 
.PP
.nf
mkdir \-p ~/local/csmp

.fi
.PP

.IP "2." 4
\fBConfigure with the installation prefix:\fP 
.PP
.nf
cd build
cmake \&.\&. \-DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=~/local/csmp \-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release

.fi
.PP

.IP "3." 4
\fBBuild and install:\fP 
.PP
.nf
make \-j$(nproc)  # For Linux
make \-j$(sysctl \-n hw\&.ncpu)  # For macOS
make install

.fi
.PP

.PP
.SS "System-wide Installation"
For system-wide installation (requires sudo privileges):

.PP
.PP
.nf
cd build
cmake \&.\&. \-DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr/local \-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
make \-j$(nproc)  # For Linux
make \-j$(sysctl \-n hw\&.ncpu)  # For macOS
sudo make install
.fi
.PP
.SS "Installation Structure"
The installation creates the following directory structure under the chosen prefix:

.PP
.PP
.nf
prefix/
├── bin/          # Executables
├── include/      # Header files
├── lib/          # Libraries
└── share/        # Documentation and other resources
.fi
.PP
.SS "Post-Installation"
To use the installed executables, add the installation's \fRbin\fP directory to your PATH:

.PP
.PP
.nf
# For user\-specific installation
echo 'export PATH=~/local/csmp/bin:$PATH' >> ~/\&.bashrc  # For bash
echo 'export PATH=~/local/csmp/bin:$PATH' >> ~/\&.zshrc   # For zsh

# For system\-wide installation
echo 'export PATH=/usr/local/bin:$PATH' >> ~/\&.bashrc    # For bash
echo 'export PATH=/usr/local/bin:$PATH' >> ~/\&.zshrc     # For zsh
.fi
.PP

.PP
After adding to PATH, either restart your terminal or run: 
.PP
.nf
source ~/\&.bashrc  # For bash
source ~/\&.zshrc   # For zsh

.fi
.PP
.SH "Building CSMP (Library, Examples and Tests) with CMake"
.PP
This guide explains how to build the CSMP project using CMake, a tool that helps manage the build process across different platforms\&. This is especially useful if you are new to building software on Linux or other Unix-like systems\&.
.SS "What is CMake?"
CMake is not a build system itself, but rather a tool that \fIgenerates\fP build system files (like Makefiles) for your specific operating system and compiler\&. This means you write a \fRCMakeLists\&.txt\fP file once, and CMake can generate build files for many different environments (like Make on Linux, Ninja, or even Xcode projects on macOS)\&.
.SS "The CMake Workflow"
The typical CMake workflow involves three main steps:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
\fBConfigure:\fP Run \fRcmake\fP in a build directory to process the \fRCMakeLists\&.txt\fP files and generate the build system (e\&.g\&., Makefiles)\&.
.IP "2." 4
\fBBuild:\fP Run the generated build system (e\&.g\&., \fRmake\fP) to compile the source code and create executables and libraries\&. There are multiple options here\&.
.IP "3." 4
\fBInstall:\fP Install the built software to a specified location\&.
.PP
.SS "Prerequisites"
Before you begin, make sure you have the following installed on your system:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBCMake:\fP You can download it from \fRcmake\&.org\fP or install it using your system's package manager (e\&.g\&., \fRsudo apt-get install cmake\fP on Ubuntu, \fRbrew install cmake\fP on macOS)\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBA C++ Compiler:\fP Such as GCC or Clang\&. Install using your system's package manager (e\&.g\&., \fRsudo apt-get install build-essential\fP on Ubuntu)\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBMake:\fP This is usually included with build tools (like \fRbuild-essential\fP)\&.
.PP
.SS "Step-by-Step Build Guide"
It is highly recommended to perform an \fBout-of-source build\fP\&. This means you build the project in a separate directory from your source code\&. This keeps your source directory clean and makes it easy to start over if something goes wrong\&.

.PP
.IP "1." 4
\fBOpen a Terminal:\fP Navigate to the root directory of the project in your terminal\&.
.IP "2." 4
\fBCreate a Build Directory:\fP Create a new directory where the build files will be generated\&. A common name is \fRbuild\fP\&. \fRbash mkdir build \fP
.IP "3." 4
\fBNavigate into the Build Directory:\fP \fRbash cd build \fP
.IP "4." 4
\fBConfigure the Project:\fP Run \fRcmake\fP from inside the \fRbuild\fP directory, pointing it to the root of your source code (which is the directory above your current location, represented by \fR\&.\&.\fP)\&.

.PP
\fRbash cmake \&.\&. \fP

.PP
By default, this will configure a \fRDebug\fP build\&. You can specify the build type using the \fR-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE\fP option:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
For a release build (optimized): \fRbash cmake \&.\&. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
For a debug build (with debugging symbols): \fRbash cmake \&.\&. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \fP
.PP

.PP
You can also enable specific options defined in the \fRCMakeLists\&.txt\fP files using the \fR-D\fP flag\&. For example:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
To enable the SAMG solver (if you a license to this commercial linear algebra package and configured it with CMake): \fRbash cmake \&.\&. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -Dsamg=ON \fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
To enable the SKM unit tests: \fRbash cmake \&.\&. -DBUILD_SKM_TESTS=ON \fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
You can combine options: \fRbash cmake \&.\&. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -Dsamg=ON -DBUCCESS_SKM_TESTS=ON \fP
.PP

.PP
After running the configure step, CMake will generate the build files in the \fRbuild\fP directory\&. You should see output indicating that the configuration was successful\&.
.IP "5." 4
\fBBuild the Project:\fP Now that the build files are generated, you can use the \fRmake\fP command (or the command for your chosen build system) to compile the code\&.

.PP
\fRbash make \fP

.PP
To speed up the build process by using multiple processor cores, you can use the \fR-j\fP flag followed by the number of cores you want to use\&. A common practice is to use all available cores:

.PP
\fRbash make -j$(nproc) # For Linux make -j$(sysctl -n hw\&.ncpu) # For macOS \fP

.PP
The build process will compile the source files and create the libraries and executables\&.
.IP "6." 4
\fBRun Tests (Optional):\fP If testing was enabled during configuration (\fR-DBUILD_TESTING=ON\fP), you can run the tests using CTest from the \fRbuild\fP directory\&.

.PP
\fRbash ctest \fP

.PP
To see more detailed output during testing, use the \fR-V\fP flag:

.PP
\fRbash ctest -V \fP
.IP "7." 4
\fBInstall the Project (Optional):\fP If an install target is defined in the \fRCMakeLists\&.txt\fP file, you can install the built project\&.

.PP
\fRbash make install \fP

.PP
You can specify an installation prefix during the CMake configure step using \fR-DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/path/to/install\fP\&.
.PP
.SS "Cleaning the Build"
If you need to clean the build directory (e\&.g\&., to start a fresh build), you can either:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
Run the clean target from the build directory: \fRbash make clean \fP
.IP "2." 4
Simply delete the \fRbuild\fP directory and create it again\&. This is often the safest way to ensure a completely clean build\&. \fRbash cd \&.\&. rm -rf build mkdir build cd build \fP
.PP

.PP
This covers the basic steps to configure and build the project using CMake\&. If you encounter specific errors, they often provide clues about missing dependencies or configuration issues\&. Much success
